  "question": {
    "text": "During the melting of a slab of ice at 273 K at atmospheric pressure :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "Pja4M",
      "text": "Internal energy of ice-water system remains unchanged.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "01PU6",
      "text": "Positive work is done by the ice-water system on the atmosphere.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "oNntk",
      "text": "Internal energy of the ice-water system decreases.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "BlIf8",
      "text": "Positive work is done on the ice-water system by the atmosphere.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Volume decreases during melting of ice so positive work is done on ice water system by atmosphere<br/>Heat absorbed by ice water so $\\Delta \\mathrm{Q}$ is positive, work done by ice water system is negative<br/>Hence by first law of thermodynamics<br/>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\Delta \\mathrm{Q}+\\Delta \\mathrm{W}=\\text { Positive }$<br/>So internal energy increases",
    "image": null
  },
